WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:05.312
Black Panther's first cut came in at nearly
two and a half hours which means there are

00:00:05.312 --> 00:00:09.640
some intriguing scenes and concepts cut from
the final movie we never got to see.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:13.789
Yippee-ki-yay movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and today I'm going to reveal details about

00:00:13.789 --> 00:00:18.230
a deleted Rocky-style montage, footage chopped
from the end of the movie, two characters

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:22.880
Ryan Coogler wanted to include in the film
but couldn't, and much more!

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.720
If you're here for the first time, I do regular
Marvel and deleted scenes videos, plus I've

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.400
got a Black Panther giveaway running right
now for all my subscribers.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.900
So, make sure you hit the bell and leave me
a comment with your thoughts about the deleted

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.470
scenes for a chance to win!

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:41.260
Obviously, spoilers ahead for Black Panther,
so take care if you haven't seen it yet.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:46.920
Black Panther's crew did an amazing job bringing
the nation of Wakanda to vivid life on screen.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:51.040
And if writer-director Ryan Coogler had had
his way, we would have seen even more of the

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.610
incredible African nation in the final film.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:57.660
Yes, early drafts of the script included a
scene where T'Challa and Nakia went for a

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.160
run through Wakanda to Nakia's home.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.930
Speaking to Digital Spy, Coogler described
the scene as a 'Rocky-style jog', pretty fitting

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:08.020
given the director's second feature film was
Creed.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:12.600
Although Black Panther had a very healthy
budget, more in fact than several of Marvel's

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:17.289
other recent films, in the end the scene was
cut to save money, though Coogler added that

00:01:17.289 --> 00:01:21.939
even if they had shot it he doubts there'd
have been time for it in the final version;

00:01:21.939 --> 00:01:25.469
I guess because it wasn't essential to the
actual plot.

00:01:25.469 --> 00:01:29.590
If the scene had made it into the movie though,
not only would we have been able to explore

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:34.179
more of the country, but we'd also have met
Nakia's parents and seen what they thought

00:01:34.179 --> 00:01:35.969
of T'Challa, their daughter's ex-boyfriend.

00:01:35.969 --> 00:01:41.619
Chadwick Boseman revealed there are a couple
of deleted scenes of T'Challa and Everett

00:01:41.619 --> 00:01:42.869
Ross in his office.

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:47.759
The scenes he's referring to are likely after
Ross wakes up and talks to Shuri and discovers

00:01:47.759 --> 00:01:51.950
that the bullet injury to his spine has been
healed with Wakandan technology.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:57.130
It's the first time Ross has ever seen Wakanda
and Shuri tells him not to touch anything

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:59.149
as her brother will return soon.

00:01:59.149 --> 00:02:02.909
I think there's a snippet of this deleted
scene in the second Black Panther trailer

00:02:02.909 --> 00:02:07.499
in the form of a voiceover by Martin Freeman
which could match the discussion he had with

00:02:07.499 --> 00:02:12.030
T'Challa in the scene that was cut.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:15.700
"I have seen gods fly."

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:19.810
"I've seen men build weapons that I couldn't
even imagine."

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:22.590
"I've seen aliens drop from the sky."

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:26.980
"But I have never seen anything like this."

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:32.180
And there's also some footage of Everett Ross
meeting with T'Challa and the Wakandan delegation

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:35.470
at the UN which we never saw in the finished
film.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:39.830
Judging by Ross's body language in this deleted
clip here, I wonder if he might be trying

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:44.060
to dissuade T'Challa from revealing the truth
about Wakanda, or at least be voicing his

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:46.700
own doubts about what the repercussions might
be?

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:50.710
Either way, the reason this footage is missing
from the theatrical release is that, according

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:54.960
to Ryan Coogler, the ending to the movie was
originally quite different.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.820
Initially, T'Challa's UN speech about opening
up Wakanda to the rest of the world was going

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.950
to be the final scene before the credits started
to roll.

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:06.980
In an interview with the Radio Times, Coogler
said, "It was a complicated thing.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.260
[…] It was one of those things where we
were working on the ending in the edit.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.880
[And] we were kind of deciding how we'd play
it.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:16.900
We knew we had the ability to do a post-credits
sequence if we wanted to."

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:21.490
Coogler also explained that, in the end, he
wanted the final scene to parallel the first

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:22.880
scene in Oakland.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:26.819
As he said, "If you think about it, the UN
scene, and the scene that happens in Oakland

00:03:26.819 --> 00:03:28.870
are basically the same scene.

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:31.120
It's a reveal of who Wakandans are.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.260
The only difference is who they're revealing
that to.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.740
And we thought that it was stronger emotionally
[to end in Oakland]."

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:42.300
Now, what Coogler says does make absolute
sense, though some Marvel fans might have

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:46.290
preferred if the UN speech had been in the
main movie, as we might have got an extra

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:47.560
end-credits scene that way.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:48.560
And, judging by Everett Ross's body language
in this deleted footage here, I also wonder

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:49.560
if, whether Ross might be trying to dissuade
T'Challa from revealing the truth about Wakanda,

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:50.560
or at least voice his own doubts about what
it might mean?

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:51.790
By the way, if you want to understand why
the ending of Black Panther is so important

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:56.160
for the MCU, plus how it sets up Avengers
Infinity War, then tap up here to watch my

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:00.950
full breakdown of the ending or click the
links in the video description below.

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:05.200
The Kings' all-female bodyguard, the Dora
Milaje play a big part in some of the movie's

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:10.930
most spectacular action sequences, with Danai
Gurira giving an awesome performance as Okoye.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:14.990
But it looks like there were still some cuts
to the Dora's screen time.

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:18.850
Last year Vanity Fair reported that in an
early rough cut of footage from the film,

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:23.180
there was a scene of the Dora Milaje dancing
on a boat which featured "[Danai] Gurira's

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:27.900
Okoye and [Florence] Kasumba's Ayo swaying
rhythmically back in formation with the rest

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.419
of their team.

00:04:29.419 --> 00:04:33.680
Okoye eyes Ayo flirtatiously for a long time
as the camera pans in on them.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.979
Eventually, she says, appreciatively and appraisingly,
'You look good.'

00:04:37.979 --> 00:04:42.289
Ayo responds in kind [and] Okoye grins and
replies, 'I know.'"

00:04:42.289 --> 00:04:46.430
While some of the dancing did make it into
the final film, the flirtation between Okoye

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:51.370
and Ayo, who in the comics has a romantic
relationship with another Dora Milaje, was

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:52.370
cut.

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:56.499
In an interview with Vulture, Ayo actress
Kasumba seemed to confirm the moment had been

00:04:56.499 --> 00:04:58.940
filmed but that it was given the chop.

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:03.180
While Kasumba couldn't confirm the reason
for its removal, she suggested it might have

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:07.300
been because the movie had a lot to do in
introducing the world of Wakanda and needed

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:08.809
to keep the focus on T'Challa.

00:05:08.809 --> 00:05:13.841
However, this isn't the first time that a
scene which hints towards an LGBTQ character

00:05:13.841 --> 00:05:18.240
has been cut from a Marvel movie, the most
recent being in Thor: Ragnarok where a scene

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.900
of a woman leaving Valkyrie's bedroom was
deleted from that film.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:26.830
In the MCU's Black Panther, Okoye is in a
relationship with W'Kabi and that ends up

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:31.260
being significant in the movie's finale; so,
I wonder whether that played any part in the

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:35.150
decision to drop the earlier moment between
Okoye and Ayo.

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:39.379
An interview with the film's co-writer Joe
Robert Cole indicates that may be the case

00:05:39.379 --> 00:05:44.669
as he suggests that they did have the LGBTQ
storyline from the comics in mind though it

00:05:44.669 --> 00:05:49.270
wouldn't have been 'a major theme' and they
also discussed many different arcs for different

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:50.270
characters.

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:54.659
Although Kasumba has said that she felt it
was important in this first film to just establish

00:05:54.659 --> 00:05:59.979
the Dora Milaje as a unit, she would still
like Ayo's sexuality to be explored in a future

00:05:59.979 --> 00:06:02.059
Black Panther movie.

00:06:02.059 --> 00:06:06.590
Michael B. Jordan's Killmonger has been rightfully
praised for bringing an excellent antagonist

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:12.169
into the MCU, however, in the early stages,
Ryan Coogler, who's a comics fan himself,

00:06:12.169 --> 00:06:15.610
had plans to introduce a different villain
into the movie.

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:19.719
That villain was Kraven the Hunter who first
appeared in The Amazing Spider-Man comics

00:06:19.719 --> 00:06:21.699
and is a big-game hunter.

00:06:21.699 --> 00:06:26.430
Coogler particularly enjoyed Christopher Priest's
run on the Black Panther comics where Panther

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:30.449
fought Kraven, however, Marvel told him he
couldn't use the character.

00:06:30.449 --> 00:06:35.320
I suspect because Kraven was introduced in
the Spider-Man comics, the character is bundled

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:37.629
up with Sony's Spider-Man rights.

00:06:37.629 --> 00:06:43.219
Obviously, we do have Spider-Man in the MCU
right now, however, that was a specific arrangement

00:06:43.219 --> 00:06:46.400
which Marvel Studios had to broker with Sony.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:50.789
Sony themselves are developing their own Spider-Man-related
universe, which appears to be separate to

00:06:50.789 --> 00:06:56.779
the MCU, with characters like Venom, Silver
Sable and Black Cat, and possibly even Kraven

00:06:56.779 --> 00:06:57.809
himself.

00:06:57.809 --> 00:07:03.900
When Ryan Coogler and Joe Robert Cole started
working on ideas for the movie, another character

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:09.509
they seriously thought about including was
Patriot, aka Elijah Bradley, who's one of

00:07:09.509 --> 00:07:11.610
the founders of the Young Avengers.

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:17.210
In the comics, Patriot is the grandson of
Isaiah Bradley, who was one of 300 African-Americans

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:20.939
soldiers used as test subjects for the Super
Soldier Serum [that created Captain America].

00:07:20.939 --> 00:07:25.460
The thing is though, Patriot isn't actually
part of Black Panther's story in the comics,

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:29.870
and that's why Coogler ultimately decided
against introducing him in the movie, as he

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:35.319
wanted to concentrate more on Wakanda and
characters who were already part of that world.

00:07:35.319 --> 00:07:39.729
Showing us the nation of Wakanda in all its
glorious detail was always going to be a central

00:07:39.729 --> 00:07:44.839
part of the Black Panther movie, but so was
travelling beyond the African country's borders

00:07:44.839 --> 00:07:49.770
as the filmmakers wanted to give a sense of
some of the effects of Wakanda's isolationism.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:55.539
But while the finished film globe-trots between
Busan, South Korea; Oakland, California; London;

00:07:55.539 --> 00:08:01.119
Vienna; and Nigeria's Sambisa Forest, an early
draft of the movie also included a sequence

00:08:01.119 --> 00:08:02.580
in Chicago.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:06.330
According to screenwriter Joe Robert Cole,
it was going to be a 'pretty big' scene, though

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:08.039
sadly he didn't elaborate.

00:08:08.039 --> 00:08:13.520
One of the standouts in Black Panther was
Letitia Wright's Shuri, tech genius and T'Challa's

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:14.550
little sister.

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:18.969
Shuri is also an interesting character in
the comics who's taken on the mantle of Black

00:08:18.969 --> 00:08:20.789
Panther herself on occasions [when T'Challa
has been unavailable].

00:08:20.789 --> 00:08:24.710
And, in fact, Letitia Wright has said she
thought Ryan Coogler had toyed with the idea

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:29.210
of setting up Shuri as a Black Panther and
I can see a hint to that in the neckpiece

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:31.889
with panther teeth that she wore in some scenes
of the movie.

00:08:31.889 --> 00:08:36.490
For the moment, I expect T'Challa will be
the only Black Panther, and Shuri's appearances

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:41.410
in Avengers: Infinity War and other crossovers
will again showcase her incredible tech skills

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:44.520
and ability to create highly advanced gadgets.

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:49.090
But perhaps when Black Panther's sequel arrives
in Phase 4, we could see Shuri put on a Black

00:08:49.090 --> 00:08:50.210
Panther suit.

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:54.830
She could either be an additional Black Panther
to T'Challa or she could take over from him,

00:08:54.830 --> 00:08:57.080
for example, if he were injured.

00:08:57.080 --> 00:08:59.650
After all there were three suits in her lab.

00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:03.840
T'Challa took one, of course, and I think
Killmonger took the gold-necklaced suit, which

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:06.410
would conveniently leave one spare for Shuri.

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:09.830
And that's without even counting any future
suits she might design.

